<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Dynasty of E</h1><p class="st-meta">979 BCE – 732 BCE (approx.) · capital Babylon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The mixed series of Babylonian kings who ruled from about 979 to 732 BCE, through Aramean upheaval and growing Assyrian domination.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dynasty of E is the conventional name for the kings who followed the Elamite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mar-biti-apla-usur">Mar-biti-apla-usur</a> on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from around 979 BCE; the king lists use an abbreviation of uncertain meaning. They were not a single family. The first, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-apli">Nabu-mukin-apli</a>, ruled at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Arameans</a> cut Babylon off and the New Year festival could not be held.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-apla-iddina">Nabu-apla-iddina</a> restored the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, commemorated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tablet-of-shamash">Sun-god Tablet</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk-zakir-shumi-i">Marduk-zakir-shumi I</a> relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> to put down his brother's revolt, a sign of growing dependence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bit-yakin">Bit-Yakin</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bit-amukani">Bit-Amukani</a> grew powerful in the south. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonassar">Nabonassar</a> (747 to 734 BCE) systematic astronomical observations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a> began, later used by Ptolemy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonassar">Nabonassar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-zeri">Nabu-mukin-zeri of Bit-Amukani</a> seized the throne in 732 BCE, and Tiglath-Pileser III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 729 BCE, beginning direct Assyrian kingship there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dynasty-of-e.jpg" alt="Dynasty of E" width="320" height="391"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harran_Stela.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>979 BCE – 732 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Babylon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nabu-apla-iddina, Marduk-zakir-shumi I, Nabonassar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Babylonian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eighth Dynasty of Babylon, Post-Kassite Babylonia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-856&amp;focus=dynasty-of-e" aria-label="Dynasty of E on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5820 995 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dynasty of E lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6140 1251L6140 1251M6140 1251L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 1251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Babylon. <a href="/atlas?year=-856&amp;focus=dynasty-of-e">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
